# Week 4: Linked Lists - Detailed Lesson Plan

## Lesson Overview
**Duration**: 2-hour lecture + 3-hour lab  
**Prerequisites**: Arrays, references/pointers in Java, basic OOP  
**Learning Outcomes**: By the end of this week, students will be able to implement linked list data structures and solve common linked list problems using pointer manipulation techniques.

---

## Part 1: Singly Linked List Fundamentals (45 minutes)

### Introduction (10 minutes)
**Hook**: "Why does inserting at the beginning of a LinkedList take O(1) time, but inserting at the beginning of an ArrayList takes O(n)? Today we'll discover the power of pointer-based data structures."

**Real-world analogy**: 
- Scavenger hunt where each clue points to the next location (linked list)
- vs. Books on a shelf where you must shift everything (array)

### Core Concepts (20 minutes)

#### Node Structure
```java
class Node {
    int data;          // The value stored
    Node next;         // Reference to next node

    public Node(int data) {
        this.data = data;
        this.next = null;
    }
}
```

**Visual Representation**:
```
Head -> [5|•] -> [10|•] -> [15|•] -> [20|null]
         data next
```

#### Basic Operations Complexity
| Operation | Array | Linked List |
|-----------|-------|-------------|
| Access by index | O(1) | O(n) |
| Insert at beginning | O(n) | O(1) |
| Insert at end | O(1) | O(n) without tail |
| Delete at beginning | O(n) | O(1) |
| Search | O(n) | O(n) |

**Key Insight**: Trade-off between random access and insertion flexibility

### Live Coding Demo (15 minutes)
**Implement basic SinglyLinkedList class**

```java
public class SinglyLinkedList {
    private Node head;
    private int size;

    // Insert at beginning - O(1)
    public void insertFirst(int data) {
        Node newNode = new Node(data);
        newNode.next = head;
        head = newNode;
        size++;
    }

    // Display list
    public void display() {
        Node current = head;
        while (current != null) {
            System.out.print(current.data + " -> ");
            current = current.next;
        }
        System.out.println("null");
    }

    // Insert at end - O(n)
    public void insertLast(int data) {
        Node newNode = new Node(data);

        if (head == null) {
            head = newNode;
            size++;
            return;
        }

        Node current = head;
        while (current.next != null) {
            current = current.next;
        }
        current.next = newNode;
        size++;
    }

    // Delete first node - O(1)
    public void deleteFirst() {
        if (head == null) {
            throw new IllegalStateException("List is empty");
        }
        head = head.next;
        size--;
    }
}
```

**Common Pitfalls to Highlight**:
1. Forgetting to check for null head
2. Losing reference to head when modifying
3. Not updating size counter
4. Infinite loops in traversal

---

## Part 2: Advanced Linked List Operations (30 minutes)

### Deletion by Value (10 minutes)

```java
public boolean delete(int value) {
    // Case 1: Empty list
    if (head == null) return false;

    // Case 2: Delete head
    if (head.data == value) {
        head = head.next;
        size--;
        return true;
    }

    // Case 3: Delete middle or end
    Node current = head;
    while (current.next != null) {
        if (current.next.data == value) {
            current.next = current.next.next;
            size--;
            return true;
        }
        current = current.next;
    }

    return false; // Value not found
}
```

**Tracing Exercise**: Draw pointer changes step-by-step

### Reverse a Linked List (20 minutes)
**Problem**: Reverse the list 1->2->3->4 to become 4->3->2->1

**Approach 1: Iterative (Recommended)**
```java
public void reverse() {
    Node prev = null;
    Node current = head;
    Node next = null;

    while (current != null) {
        next = current.next;    // Save next
        current.next = prev;    // Reverse link
        prev = current;         // Move prev forward
        current = next;         // Move current forward
    }

    head = prev; // Update head
}
```

**Visual Step-by-Step**:
```
Initial:  null <- prev  current -> next
                    1  ->  2  ->  3  -> null

Step 1:   null <- 1    current -> next
           prev           2  ->  3  -> null

Step 2:   null <- 1 <- 2    current -> next
                    prev       3  -> null

Step 3:   null <- 1 <- 2 <- 3    current
                         prev     (null)
```

**Approach 2: Recursive** (Challenge for advanced students)
```java
public void reverseRecursive() {
    head = reverseHelper(head);
}

private Node reverseHelper(Node node) {
    // Base case
    if (node == null || node.next == null) {
        return node;
    }

    // Recursive case
    Node newHead = reverseHelper(node.next);
    node.next.next = node;  // Reverse the link
    node.next = null;        // Break old link

    return newHead;
}
```

